WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.050
- This video was made
possible by Tab for a Cause.

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:04.670
Raise money for charity just by opening

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:05.950
tabs in your browser.

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:07.090
With the Mueller Report out,

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:08.928
we finally have some answers
about Russian efforts

00:00:08.928 --> 00:00:11.720
to interfere with the 2016 election

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:13.220
and the administration's efforts

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:14.999
to stymie that investigation.

00:00:14.999 --> 00:00:18.760
But some of our original
questions remain unanswered.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.096
The report raises new questions

00:00:20.096 --> 00:00:22.737
and now we have to start
thinking about next steps.

00:00:22.737 --> 00:00:25.154
(horn music)

00:00:26.381 --> 00:00:28.235
Hey, Legal Eagles, today we're talking

00:00:28.235 --> 00:00:30.662
about the known unknowns that remain

00:00:30.662 --> 00:00:33.630
in the wake of the
redacted Mueller Report.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.064
As we discussed in a prior video,

00:00:35.064 --> 00:00:37.265
Robert Mueller created a legal framework

00:00:37.265 --> 00:00:39.081
that would not have allowed him

00:00:39.081 --> 00:00:41.830
to indict a sitting president

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:43.735
or to levy formal charges
against the president

00:00:43.735 --> 00:00:44.940
on fairness grounds.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.450
The issue from a legal perspective

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:49.682
is we don't know if Mr. Mueller is correct

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:51.932
about the unindictability
of the president.

00:00:51.932 --> 00:00:54.561
This is largely a constitutional question.

00:00:54.561 --> 00:00:57.885
We have memos from the
Office of Legal Counsel,

00:00:57.885 --> 00:01:00.376
the OLC, that talk about this,

00:01:00.376 --> 00:01:02.152
but the OLC memos themselves

00:01:02.152 --> 00:01:04.720
are not consistent on this point.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:06.760
And the OLC itself is an
arm of the executive branch

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.609
so it would not be surprising

00:01:09.609 --> 00:01:11.577
if they took the view that their boss

00:01:11.577 --> 00:01:16.577
cannot be indicted while
sitting in the executive office.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:19.530
The only way to know
if a sitting president

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:22.593
can in fact be indicted
or to have formal charges

00:01:22.593 --> 00:01:23.981
levied against them is

00:01:23.981 --> 00:01:26.963
to test it at the Supreme Court.

00:01:26.963 --> 00:01:29.314
And Mueller's framework that he created

00:01:29.314 --> 00:01:31.229
certainly doesn't allow
for this possibility

00:01:31.229 --> 00:01:32.744
which leads us to the second open question

00:01:32.744 --> 00:01:36.448
which is what about the
statue of limitations

00:01:36.448 --> 00:01:41.400
during the pendency of
a president's tenure?

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.270
Mueller takes the view that he cannot

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:45.980
indict a sitting president.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.170
We don't know if he
actually would have indicted

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:50.530
the president, but he takes the position

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:53.230
that he is not even going
to get to that question.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.076
But because we don't know
if Mueller is correct,

00:01:55.076 --> 00:01:57.460
we also don't know what happens

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:00.334
to potential crimes that
a president committed

00:02:00.334 --> 00:02:02.600
before taking office.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:03.847
Generally speaking, most crimes

00:02:03.847 --> 00:02:06.242
have a statute of limitations.

00:02:06.242 --> 00:02:08.351
It's in society's interest for most things

00:02:08.351 --> 00:02:10.660
to be able to lay to rest

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:13.110
certain offenses that happened so long ago

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:15.360
that it would be impractical to try

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.170
and get a conviction years
or decades after the fact.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.715
So, there's a sun-setting provision

00:02:21.715 --> 00:02:23.440
after certain offenses have happened.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:25.605
It's not true for all crimes,

00:02:25.605 --> 00:02:26.540
but it certainly applies to most of things

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.327
that you can imagine a
president having done

00:02:28.327 --> 00:02:31.060
before or during office.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.400
With respect to obstruction of justice,

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:37.413
section 3282 of title 18 of the US Code

00:02:37.413 --> 00:02:40.000
is the general statute of limitations

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.978
that applies when there
isn't a more specific

00:02:41.978 --> 00:02:44.260
statute of limitations.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:46.127
It states that "Except as otherwise

00:02:46.127 --> 00:02:47.503
"expressly provided by law,

00:02:47.503 --> 00:02:50.387
"a prosecution for non-capital offense

00:02:50.387 --> 00:02:52.187
"shall be instituted within five years

00:02:52.187 --> 00:02:53.980
"after the offense was committed."

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:58.220
So, unless there is a more
specific statute of limitations

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:01.270
in the federal system, it's
generally a five-year statute.

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:02.467
So, practically speaking, even

00:03:02.467 --> 00:03:05.450
if a sitting president committed the crime

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:06.920
of obstruction of justice,

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:09.730
what happens if the president is president

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:10.990
for more than five years?

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:13.120
What if the president wins a second term?

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.620
And has committed this particular crime.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:17.297
Theoretically, if a president committed

00:03:17.297 --> 00:03:19.090
the crime of obstruction of justice

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:20.970
and then won his second term,

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:23.020
then becoming president could be like

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:24.840
a get out of jail free card

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.270
which I don't think anyone would support.

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:28.810
And that also raises the questions

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:31.020
about offenses that
might have been committed

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.830
before taking office.

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:34.490
Those offenses may not have

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:36.180
a five-year statute of limitations.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:39.200
There could be a two or
four statute of limitations

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:41.160
for fraud-related offenses.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:42.670
Or there could be a very specific

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:44.330
one-year statute of limitations,

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:46.776
as sometimes happens in statutory law.

00:03:46.776 --> 00:03:50.320
This is an untested part of the law.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:53.760
Robert Mueller's position
is perfectly reasonable

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.250
that you cannot indict
a sitting president,

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:57.700
at least criminally speaking.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:03:59.291
And the corollary to that argument

00:03:59.291 --> 00:04:00.880
would probably be that well,

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.164
you can't actually indict
the sitting president

00:04:03.164 --> 00:04:05.120
with the crime, that means

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:06.160
that the statute of limitations

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:08.670
should be told for that period of time,

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:10.047
but it's entirely possible
that if the question

00:04:10.047 --> 00:04:12.206
ever got to the Supreme Court,

00:04:12.206 --> 00:04:13.500
the Supreme Court says,

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:14.776
no, no, no, Mr. Mueller was incorrect,

00:04:14.776 --> 00:04:16.690
you can indict a sitting president

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:18.670
and as a result, the
statute of limitations

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:21.160
is not told and also, by the way,

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:23.110
you waited too long to bring the crime

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:24.920
and therefore, the statute of limitations

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:26.312
has expired and the
president can no longer

00:04:26.312 --> 00:04:28.221
be charged with this offense,

00:04:28.221 --> 00:04:31.140
even after leaving the presidency.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:32.581
That's the problem with
having these untested

00:04:32.581 --> 00:04:35.745
constitutional questions that only

00:04:35.745 --> 00:04:39.360
the Supreme Court can
answer in this context.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.680
Which brings us to the next open question.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.759
We don't know to what degree of certainty

00:04:43.759 --> 00:04:46.040
Mr. Mueller thinks that the president

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:47.931
may or may not have committed the crime

00:04:47.931 --> 00:04:50.029
of obstruction of justice

00:04:50.029 --> 00:04:53.300
and knowing to what degree of certainty

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:55.057
the evidence rises would probably

00:04:55.057 --> 00:04:58.020
help the electorate
and members of congress

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:00.130
to know what they should be doing

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:01.570
in terms of next steps.

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:03.100
While the Mueller Report itself

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:05.033
is often silent about how strong

00:05:05.033 --> 00:05:07.550
Mueller thinks the evidence is,

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:09.703
Attorney General Barr
in his press conference

00:05:09.703 --> 00:05:11.840
shortly before the redacted Mueller Report

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.818
was released, said that Mueller told him

00:05:14.818 --> 00:05:17.302
in person that he wouldn't have found

00:05:17.302 --> 00:05:19.034
a crime even in the absence

00:05:19.034 --> 00:05:21.244
of the legal limitations that were created

00:05:21.244 --> 00:05:23.520
as a result of the framework

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:24.896
under which Mueller operated.

00:05:24.896 --> 00:05:27.496
- On March 5th, we specifically asked him

00:05:27.496 --> 00:05:30.219
about the OLC opinion and whether or not

00:05:30.219 --> 00:05:33.395
he was taking the position

00:05:33.395 --> 00:05:36.310
that he would have found a crime

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:40.097
but for the existence of the OLC opinion

00:05:40.097 --> 00:05:41.622
and he made it very clear,

00:05:41.622 --> 00:05:46.370
several times that that
was not his position.

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:49.250
Frankly, given the descriptions

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:52.088
of the factual evidence
regarding obstruction,

00:05:52.088 --> 00:05:55.880
it's hard to believe that
Mueller actually said

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:59.810
he would not have made
an actual determination.

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:00.973
In fact, the Mueller Report itself

00:06:00.973 --> 00:06:02.657
says that it goes to great pains

00:06:02.657 --> 00:06:06.330
to avoid the traditional
prosecutorial decisions,

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:08.303
and in fact, the Mueller Report says

00:06:08.303 --> 00:06:11.962
that while he did find
evidence of conspiracy

00:06:11.962 --> 00:06:14.810
to collude with Russians,

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:16.910
that evidence did not rise to the level

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:18.500
of a chargeable offense.

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:20.252
So if Mueller was capable and willing

00:06:20.252 --> 00:06:22.700
to find that the evidence did not rise

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:24.543
to the level of a chargeable conspiracy

00:06:24.543 --> 00:06:27.493
with respect to Volume
1 of the Mueller Report,

00:06:27.493 --> 00:06:29.440
it doesn't make any sense

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:31.234
that he would have in secret

00:06:31.234 --> 00:06:33.830
felt the same way about Volume 2,

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:35.082
the obstruction offenses,

00:06:35.082 --> 00:06:38.123
but left it out of the
Mueller Report itself

00:06:38.123 --> 00:06:41.299
and only told the Attorney
General William Barr.

00:06:41.299 --> 00:06:43.894
That frankly does not sound accurate.

00:06:43.894 --> 00:06:46.740
Which leads me to the next open question

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:48.098
about Attorney General Barr.

00:06:48.098 --> 00:06:51.093
In Barr's four-page summary
of the Mueller Report,

00:06:51.093 --> 00:06:53.550
Attorney General Barr states

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:58.040
that he along with Deputy
Attorney General Rod Rosenstein

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:00.600
looked at the evidence that Robert Mueller

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:02.684
created regarding obstruction
of justice and came

00:07:02.684 --> 00:07:07.450
to a determination that
there was no obstruction.

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:09.546
We don't know how Barr and Rosenstein

00:07:09.546 --> 00:07:12.320
came to this determination.

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:14.120
And we also don't know if it was based

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:16.500
on Barr's view of the evidence and facts,

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:18.071
or whether it was specifically

00:07:18.071 --> 00:07:22.600
based on Barr's view of the
president's Article 2 power.

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:24.237
As we covered in a prior video,

00:07:24.237 --> 00:07:26.711
Barr has a fairly idiosyncratic view

00:07:26.711 --> 00:07:28.429
that if the president is exercising

00:07:28.429 --> 00:07:31.290
his Article 2 power,

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:34.560
it cannot possibly give rise to the crime

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:36.120
of obstruction of justice.

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:38.870
The exercise of that power is effectively

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:41.060
unchecked by the judiciary.

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:42.330
There are some scholars that agree

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:43.390
with that view of power,

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:45.610
but most people disagree.

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:48.603
So it's important to know
how Barr and Rosenstein

00:07:48.603 --> 00:07:50.050
came to the conclusion

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:52.270
that there was no obstruction of justice.

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:53.722
We also don't know what is behind

00:07:53.722 --> 00:07:57.500
the redactions of the
redacted Mueller Report.

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:01.570
By one count, there were
855 separate redactions

00:08:01.570 --> 00:08:04.589
on the basis of things like
harm to ongoing matters

00:08:04.589 --> 00:08:08.930
and grand jury testimony that
is protected by Rule 6(e).

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:10.330
On the other hand, this is not

00:08:10.330 --> 00:08:11.945
the most redacted
document I have ever seen

00:08:11.945 --> 00:08:13.220
in my legal career.

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:16.006
I've seen documents that are
far more blacked out than this.

00:08:16.006 --> 00:08:18.387
And it's hard to argue
that redacting information

00:08:18.387 --> 00:08:20.288
on the basis that it's going to harm

00:08:20.288 --> 00:08:24.854
an ongoing criminal investigation
is a bad thing to do.

00:08:24.854 --> 00:08:28.350
But on the other hand, given the disparity

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:31.110
between the Barr four-page summary

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:33.920
and the actual redacted Mueller Report,

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:35.738
I'm not sure that this Justice Department

00:08:35.738 --> 00:08:37.850
deserves the benefit of the doubt

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:40.409
with respect to the
redactions that were made.

00:08:40.409 --> 00:08:42.500
So, it is an open question

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:45.550
as to what is underneath
all of these redactions

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:48.910
and what we will see in the
weeks and months going forward

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:51.200
as it is tested in the courts

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:53.360
whether this information can be unredacted

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:54.728
and released to the public or to Congress.

00:08:54.728 --> 00:08:57.160
Additionally, in one of the appendices

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:58.727
to the redacted Mueller Report,

00:08:58.727 --> 00:09:01.115
there are 12 referrals that started

00:09:01.115 --> 00:09:03.499
with the Mueller investigation

00:09:03.499 --> 00:09:06.572
that were made to other
US Attorneys offices

00:09:06.572 --> 00:09:08.450
throughout the country.

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:11.890
Many of those referrals
are completely unknown.

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:14.490
It is blacked out what the referral was,

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:15.570
what the subject matter was,

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:19.470
and we have no idea what is going to come

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:21.740
of these investigations that started

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:22.760
with the Mueller Report,

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:25.900
but were farmed out to the
other US Attorneys offices

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:28.900
throughout the country that
have subject matter jurisdiction

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:31.047
over the rest of these
criminal investigations.

00:09:31.047 --> 00:09:33.855
Now in terms of some of
the questionable decisions

00:09:33.855 --> 00:09:35.930
that the Mueller team made,

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:37.440
at least from a legal perspective,

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:39.720
we've already talked about the legal

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:41.750
framework that Mueller created

00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:43.898
that may or may not be
constitutionally mandated,

00:09:43.898 --> 00:09:46.132
but one of the things
that really sticks out

00:09:46.132 --> 00:09:49.320
as an attorney, is Mueller's decision

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:51.051
not to interview the president.

00:09:51.051 --> 00:09:53.560
Mueller stated that he viewed his role

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:55.870
in this investigation as a fact-finder.

00:09:55.870 --> 00:09:58.620
Not to come to a conclusion about

00:09:58.620 --> 00:09:59.790
whether a sitting president

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:01.400
has committed a crime or not,

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:04.260
but to be able to gather
the relevant evidence

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:05.580
while it was still fresh,

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:07.890
while the witnesses were still available,

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:09.152
to be able to compile that

00:10:09.152 --> 00:10:11.900
so that other people and other entities

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:14.230
would be able to make
those determinations.

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:16.910
So given that, it is odd
that the Mueller team

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:19.150
decided not to interview the president.

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:20.798
Again, that might have
been the right decision,

00:10:20.798 --> 00:10:23.272
but it is an odd one in the context

00:10:23.272 --> 00:10:25.613
of a fact-gathering mission.

00:10:25.613 --> 00:10:28.618
So, instead of interviewing or deposing

00:10:28.618 --> 00:10:29.987
the president, the Mueller team

00:10:29.987 --> 00:10:32.255
decided to issue written questions

00:10:32.255 --> 00:10:36.740
that the president was able
to respond to in writing,

00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:40.320
having had his attorneys
look over all of his answers.

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:41.928
I tweeted out after the Mueller Report

00:10:41.928 --> 00:10:44.530
was issued that depositions

00:10:44.530 --> 00:10:46.140
are better than interrogatories.

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:47.389
And what I mean by that
is in the civil world,

00:10:47.389 --> 00:10:50.759
at least, you have mechanisms in discovery

00:10:50.759 --> 00:10:52.299
to be able to interview witnesses,

00:10:52.299 --> 00:10:54.894
or to ask them written questions.

00:10:54.894 --> 00:10:56.904
Written questions are
called interrogatories

00:10:56.904 --> 00:11:00.010
and these interrogatories can be useful,

00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:01.531
but the problem is that the opposing side

00:11:01.531 --> 00:11:05.397
will review it dozens of times

00:11:05.397 --> 00:11:07.407
through their attorneys
so that the information

00:11:07.407 --> 00:11:11.603
that you receive is not
particularly useful.

00:11:11.603 --> 00:11:14.300
Getting someone in front of a camera,

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:16.830
in front of a stenographer under oath,

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:18.268
and asking them oral questions

00:11:18.268 --> 00:11:22.530
is almost always a far better way

00:11:22.530 --> 00:11:24.480
to understand what they were thinking,

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:26.540
why they did certain actions

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:29.010
and to actually get at the truth.

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:30.100
Credit where credit is due,

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:31.911
I would say it was a very smart tactic

00:11:31.911 --> 00:11:33.603
of President Trump's attorneys

00:11:33.603 --> 00:11:35.855
to prevent and delay him from sitting

00:11:35.855 --> 00:11:38.641
in front of an oral deposition

00:11:38.641 --> 00:11:41.655
and in issuing these written answers

00:11:41.655 --> 00:11:43.700
that are very, very safe,

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:45.308
and basically don't say anything

00:11:45.308 --> 00:11:47.710
that could incriminate the president.

00:11:47.710 --> 00:11:49.180
It was good legal advice.

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:50.870
We, of course, knew that the president

00:11:50.870 --> 00:11:52.160
was given written questions,

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:54.301
but until now, we were
not able to see them.

00:11:54.301 --> 00:11:56.559
Now, we can finally see the questions

00:11:56.559 --> 00:12:00.180
and answers that are listed in Appendix C

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:01.551
to the redacted Mueller Report.

00:12:01.551 --> 00:12:04.279
Almost all of the president's answers

00:12:04.279 --> 00:12:06.983
come in the form of I don't know

00:12:06.983 --> 00:12:09.700
or I don't recall, which is of course,

00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:12.330
a very, very safe thing to do.

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:14.183
It is hard to get locked down

00:12:14.183 --> 00:12:16.840
in terms of lying under oath

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:18.664
or committing perjury
when you're simply saying

00:12:18.664 --> 00:12:20.709
that you don't have a
specific recollection

00:12:20.709 --> 00:12:21.878
of the thing that is asked of you.

00:12:21.878 --> 00:12:25.650
The amount of I don't
knows and I don't recalls

00:12:25.650 --> 00:12:27.080
is interesting also, for the fact,

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:28.306
that the president has claimed to have

00:12:28.306 --> 00:12:31.390
one of the greatest memories
in the entire world.

00:12:31.390 --> 00:12:33.450
- One of the great memories of all time.

00:12:33.450 --> 00:12:36.357
- But that's the issue with
getting written answers.

00:12:36.357 --> 00:12:38.730
They aren't written by the answerer,

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:40.290
they are written by the lawyers

00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:42.960
and simply signed onto by the person

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:44.290
who is asked the questions.

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:45.654
But Mr. Mueller says that he found

00:12:45.654 --> 00:12:48.450
Trump's written answers
to be, quote, inadequate.

00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:50.870
And says that the special counsel

00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:53.950
had the power to subpoena the president

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:55.850
to make him sit for deposition.

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:58.690
But Mueller refrained from doing so

00:12:58.690 --> 00:13:00.691
because issuing a subpoena
would oppose, quote,

00:13:00.691 --> 00:13:03.064
substantial delay, and he felt

00:13:03.064 --> 00:13:06.036
like he already had enough information.

00:13:06.036 --> 00:13:08.440
Those are not unreasonable concerns,

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:09.320
but given the circumstances,

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:12.170
this would have been a
very difficult decision

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:15.680
and only time will tell
whether it was the right one.

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:16.819
So in the wake of the Mueller Report,

00:13:16.819 --> 00:13:19.125
let's talk about next steps.

00:13:19.125 --> 00:13:22.792
Mueller recognizes that
impeachment is the preferred

00:13:22.792 --> 00:13:25.085
and constitutionally-sanctioned
mechanism here.

00:13:25.085 --> 00:13:27.700
He specifically states that Congress

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:30.556
can in fact police
wrong-doing by the executive,

00:13:30.556 --> 00:13:34.000
whether the allegations
of the Mueller Report

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:35.630
rise to the level of impeachment

00:13:35.630 --> 00:13:38.260
is a political question
that I will leave to you.

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:40.010
But Mueller and his team concluded

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:41.210
that Congress does, in fact,

00:13:41.210 --> 00:13:42.790
have the authority to police
obstruction of justice,

00:13:42.790 --> 00:13:45.277
stating, "We concluded that Congress

00:13:45.277 --> 00:13:47.567
"has the authority to
prohibit a president's

00:13:47.567 --> 00:13:49.047
"corrupt use of his authority

00:13:49.047 --> 00:13:50.427
"in order to protect the integrity

00:13:50.427 --> 00:13:52.940
"of the administration of justice."

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:54.230
I think it's clear that the reason

00:13:54.230 --> 00:13:55.520
that this is in the Mueller Report

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:58.787
is that this is a rebuke
of Attorney General Barr's

00:13:58.787 --> 00:14:01.860
view that you can't use the president's

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:04.140
inherent authority as a predicate

00:14:04.140 --> 00:14:05.208
for obstruction of justice.

00:14:05.208 --> 00:14:07.752
Mueller and his team did
not buy that argument.

00:14:07.752 --> 00:14:09.793
Moreover, Mueller recognizes

00:14:09.793 --> 00:14:11.850
that actual criminal prosecution

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:14.109
is an option after the
president leaves office,

00:14:14.109 --> 00:14:18.165
as does the OLC memo
on which the DOJ policy

00:14:18.165 --> 00:14:19.513
not to indict a sitting
president is based.

00:14:19.513 --> 00:14:22.887
Specifically, that quote,
"The president does not have

00:14:22.887 --> 00:14:25.997
"immunity after he leaves office."

00:14:25.997 --> 00:14:27.753
This is a further rebuke of Barr's view

00:14:27.753 --> 00:14:31.580
about the constitutional unitary

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:33.247
executive defense that would otherwise

00:14:33.247 --> 00:14:35.810
have prevented a sitting president

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:37.100
from facing charges related to actions

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:40.550
that he or she took while in office.

00:14:40.550 --> 00:14:42.793
House Judiciary Chair Jerry Nadler

00:14:42.793 --> 00:14:44.614
has issued a subpoena for
the full Mueller Report

00:14:44.614 --> 00:14:47.290
and the underlying evidence related

00:14:47.290 --> 00:14:48.355
to the investigation.

00:14:48.355 --> 00:14:50.729
It remains to be seen
what could be contained

00:14:50.729 --> 00:14:53.170
in those redacted portions.

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:54.280
And of course, we've talked

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:55.530
about the referrals that are out.

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:57.335
There are dozens of cases
that were referred out

00:14:57.335 --> 00:14:59.008
and we don't even know
the first thing about

00:14:59.008 --> 00:15:01.700
the nature of those cases.

00:15:01.700 --> 00:15:04.060
There are also rumblings
that a few Democratic

00:15:04.060 --> 00:15:05.789
members of the House are pushing
for impeachment hearings.

00:15:05.789 --> 00:15:08.330
Whether this is a good political move

00:15:08.330 --> 00:15:11.282
or whether impeachment even
has a chance of passing

00:15:11.282 --> 00:15:13.890
is beyond the scope of this channel

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:14.920
and that's a political question

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:16.440
that I will leave to you.

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:18.056
But here's what we can know for sure

00:15:18.056 --> 00:15:19.670
about the next steps.

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:21.670
Number one, the courts will decide

00:15:21.670 --> 00:15:24.500
whether to release the
unredacted Mueller Report

00:15:24.500 --> 00:15:25.651
and the underlying evidence.

00:15:25.651 --> 00:15:28.613
Number two, the dozen or so referrals

00:15:28.613 --> 00:15:30.600
to the other US Attorneys offices

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:33.091
will likely in further indictments

00:15:33.091 --> 00:15:36.487
of whom, it's anyone's guess.

00:15:36.487 --> 00:15:38.480
But recall that one of those referrals

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:40.490
led to the president's personal attorney's

00:15:40.490 --> 00:15:43.250
office being searched
and that's a huge deal.

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:44.640
It's likely that at least some

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:46.160
other people will be indicted.

00:15:46.160 --> 00:15:48.240
And number three, elected representatives

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:49.550
will have to decide whether

00:15:49.550 --> 00:15:51.810
to move forward with impeachment.

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:53.820
Impeachment is a political tool.

00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:55.574
And it will be up to the
politicians to decide

00:15:55.574 --> 00:15:58.370
whether to use that measure or not.

00:15:58.370 --> 00:15:59.880
One other potential next step

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:02.110
is perhaps do some good for this world.

